
.TH "WSLVIEW" "1" "DATEPLACEHOLDER" "VERSIONPLACEHOLDER" "WSL Utilities User Manual"
.SH NAME
.B wslview, wview, wslstart, wstart
- Component of WSL Utilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wslview
.RB [ OPTIONS ]
.I LINK_OR_FILE
.RI [ LINK_OR_FILE ...]
.PP
.B wslview \-\-reveal \fIPATH\fR
.PP
.B wslview \-\-on \fITARGET\fR
.RB [ OPTIONS ]
.I LINK_OR_FILE
.RI [ LINK_OR_FILE ...]
.PP
.B wslview \-\-on \fITARGET\fR \-\-stop
.PP
.B wslview \-\-on \fITARGET\fR \-\-reveal \fIPATH\fR
.PP
.B wslview
.BR \-r | \-u | \-e
.PP
.B wslview
.RB [ \-hv ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is a file viewer on WSL that allows you to open files and folders from WSL in Windows and a fake web browser that allows opening urls in your default browser on Windows 10. Multiple \fILINK_OR_FILE\fR arguments are accepted; every argument is opened separately.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -h, --help
print a simple help.
.TP
.B -v, --version
print current version.
.TP
.B -E, --engine \fIENGINE\fR
Select \fBpowershell\fR, \fBcmd\fR, or \fBcmd_explorer\fR as the Windows launcher.
.TP
.B -s, --skip-validation-check
Accepted for compatibility and ignored. wslview used to send a curl HTTP HEAD probe before opening a URL, but the result never changed the outcome: a URL is opened exactly as given either way, so the probe was removed.
.TP
.B --reveal \fIPATH\fR
Highlight \fIPATH\fR in its Windows Explorer folder instead of opening it. \fIPATH\fR must be an existing Linux path or a Windows path; this option cannot be combined with \fILINK_OR_FILE\fR.
.TP
.B --on \fITARGET\fR
Open on another machine (the target) instead of the local Windows desktop. \fITARGET\fR is passed to \fBssh\fR verbatim, typically an alias from ~/.ssh/config whose login lands in the target WSL instance. A URL is opened on the target as given; a single file is copied to an inbox on the target and opened there with its associated Windows application; a directory is opened in a web file browser on the target. See the REMOTE OPEN section.
.TP
.B --stop
With --on \fITARGET\fR: close the tunnels, stop the file server, and clear the registrations. Accepts no LINK_OR_FILE, --reveal, or launch options. Copied files in the target inbox are kept (see \fBWSLVIEW_ON_INBOX_TTL\fR).
.TP
.B -u, --unreg-as-browser
Remove wslview browser registration. This action accepts no LINK_OR_FILE or launch options.
.TP
.B -r, --reg-as-browser
Register wslview through update-alternatives. This action accepts no LINK_OR_FILE or launch options.
.TP
.B -e, --export-as-browser
Set BROWSER in existing .bashrc, .zshrc, and .kshrc files. This action accepts no LINK_OR_FILE or launch options.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B WSLVIEW_DEFAULT_ENGINE
default method to start websites/folders/files. Default type is "powershell". Here are all available options:
.nf
.in +4
powershell: use the Windows Shell application association through powershell.exe
cmd: compatibility alias for the PowerShell shell launcher
cmd_explorer: use explorer.exe directly
.in
.fi
.TP
.B WSLVIEW_SKIP_VALIDATION_CHECK
accepted for compatibility and ignored. It is still validated, so it must be `0` or `1`, but it no longer selects anything.
.TP
.B WSLVIEW_USE_HELPER
whether to launch through the resident helper that ships with wslu. Default is "true". Can be either `true` or `false`. Set to "false" to always launch \fBpowershell.exe\fR directly.
.TP
.B WSLVIEW_ON_PORT
the loopback port of the file server and of the ssh reverse tunnel. Change it when the port is taken on either side. Default is 8090.
.TP
.B WSLVIEW_ON_INBOX_TTL
days a copied file may stay in the target inbox (\fB~/.cache/wslu/inbox\fR) before a later copy call prunes it. Nothing else removes copied files from the target. Default is 7.
.SH NOTES
Opening a file stored in the Linux filesystem launches it through the
.B \e\ewsl.localhost\e\eDISTRO\e\e
network share. Windows treats this share as a location outside the local network, so opening such a file may show an
"Open File - Security Warning" dialog. To stop the prompt, add the share host to the Local intranet zone once per Windows user:
.PP
.RS
.nf
reg.exe add "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\\ZoneMap\\Domains\\wsl.localhost" /v file /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use
.B wsl$
instead of
.B wsl.localhost
for setups still relying on the legacy share name. Revert by deleting the added registry value.
.SH HELPER
wslview normally keeps a small helper process alive on the Windows side. The helper
holds a
.B powershell.exe
process and its Shell.Application COM object open, so a launch costs one loopback
socket round trip instead of a whole PowerShell startup. On a typical machine that
turns a launch from several hundred milliseconds into roughly 150ms.
.PP
The helper is started on demand and never delays a launch: when it is not running,
the current call goes the usual way and a helper is started in the background, so
only the first launch after it is missing is slow. It exits after two hours without
a request.
.PP
Windows only lets a process that recently received input bring a new window to
the foreground; windows opened from a background process merely flash in the
taskbar instead. Because both the helper and the fallback launcher run in the
background, they tap an unassigned key (F24 or F16) on the Windows side right
before opening anything. That grants them the foreground right, so opened
windows take the foreground as usual.
.PP
The helper only listens on the Windows loopback interface, which WSL can reach in
mirrored networking mode. In NAT mode it cannot, so the helper is simply never used
and wslview keeps launching
.B powershell.exe
itself. Whenever the helper cannot be reached for any reason, wslview falls back to
launching
.B powershell.exe
directly, so a broken or missing helper cannot break wslview.
.PP
wslview forgets a running helper when
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/wslu/wslview-helper.port
and
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/wslu/wslview-helper.token
are deleted; the process itself exits after two idle hours. Set
.B WSLVIEW_USE_HELPER
to "false" to disable the helper entirely.
.SH REMOTE OPEN
With --on, wslview opens things on the desktop of another machine (the target) instead of the local one. The target is reached over ssh between the WSL instances; in mirrored networking the ssh login on the target machine's IP lands in the target WSL instance. Paths are resolved in the local distro (a Windows path refers to the local Windows filesystem) and are never sent as paths: what crosses ssh is either a copied file or a URL.
.PP
Directories and --reveal become URLs of a small web file server (copyparty) that wslview keeps on the loopback interface of the local distro. An ssh reverse tunnel carries that port to the target, so the server is reachable only from targets of explicit --on calls. Every directory opened is registered with the file server while it runs and one server serves them all; --on \fITARGET\fR --stop clears the registrations and all file server state. Browsing needs the copyparty binary on PATH on the originating machine and wslview installed on the target. The tunnel and the server run until --stop, the network drops, or a reboot; the next --on call starts whatever is missing again. Browsing URLs work only while the file server runs; reopening a directory later gives a fresh session.
.PP
A single file is copied to \fB~/.cache/wslu/inbox\fR on the target and opened there with its associated Windows application. A file clicked inside the browser view is previewed or downloaded by the browser instead; only a copied file opens in a Windows application. Neither side attaches any filesystem, and no path on the target is ever involved.
.PP
A directory opened as an argument is shared read-write: the web file server accepts uploads into it (drag and drop, or the upload button), while moving and deleting is not possible. A directory shown by --reveal is shared read-only. Anything shared this way is browsable and writable from the browser on the target.

